    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full suite is green (6 tests, 0 failures).\n\n## What I did\n\nMoved every date-derived value onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts 1 July, identified by the calendar year in which it ends, e.g. 1 Jul 2025 \u2013 30 Jun 2026 = **FY26**).\n\n**New single source of truth \u2014 `model/FiscalYear.java`**\nPure fiscal-year arithmetic that all three consumers reuse:\n- `endingYear(date)` \u2014 the fiscal year containing a date (July+ rolls to next calendar year)\n- `label(date)` \u2192 `\"FY<YY>\"`, `twoDigitYear(date)` \u2192 the shared `<YY>` segment\n- `yearsBetween(from, to)` \u2014 whole elapsed fiscal years (July-1 boundaries crossed), never negative\n\n**Changed behaviour, each in its own small unit:**\n- `MembershipNumber` \u2014 year segment now uses `FiscalYear.twoDigitYear(...)` instead of the raw calendar year, so `-M<YY>` is the fiscal year.\n- `Tenure` \u2014 repurposed `inDays` \u2192 `inFiscalYears`, delegating to `FiscalYear.yearsBetween(...)`; tenure is now elapsed fiscal years, not 365-day windows.\n- `MembershipPoints` \u2014 the tenure-points rule now triggers on more than one elapsed fiscal year (`TENURE_DAYS`/365 \u2192 `TENURE_FISCAL_YEARS`/1).\n- `OwnerMapper` \u2014 added a `fiscalYear(owner)` mapping returning `FiscalYear.label(registrationDate)`.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `fiscalYear` property (`FY<YY>`) so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it, and updated the `membershipNumber`/`membershipPoints` descriptions.\n\nBecause `ResolveOwnerRegistrationDate` already rolls the registration date to the next business day before it is stored, `fiscalYear`, the membership number's year segment, and tenure are all derived from the business-day-adjusted date. The `FY`/`M` segments share one code path, so they can never diverge \u2014 verified by the audit line (`registrationDate=2026-09-17` \u2192 `FY27`, `-M27`).",
